cleared=[
C([1],"L2","Ethereum: every path that starts a call whose sender is not set by a signature or by the calling frame, and whether an ordinary account can make that sender the PoolManager.",
 "None can. The only such calls are the EIP-4788, EIP-2935 and request queue system calls (including the builder deposit and exit queues in go-ethereum master), each with From params.SystemAddress 0xfffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffe and a fixed target (core/state_processor.go lines 379-395, 411-425, 465-480; params/protocol_params.go line 246). Signed transactions must come from an account without code other than a delegation (core/state_transition.go preCheck, line 602). An EIP-7702 authority is recovered from its signature (core/types/tx_setcode.go lines 145-165) and is rejected if it holds code that is not a delegation (core/state_transition.go lines 1144-1146), so the PoolManager, with 24,009 bytes of code and nonce 1, can never be an authority.",
 GETH+"core/state_processor.go; "+GETH+"core/state_transition.go; "+GETH+"core/types/tx_setcode.go; "+WD+"pm_nonces.json"),
C([137],"L2","Polygon: bor state sync (commitState), span commits and any other system message, and whether their sender can be the PoolManager.",
 "No. Every system message is built by GetSystemMessage with From params.BorSystemAddress 0xffffFFFfFFffffffffffffffFfFFFfffFFFfFFfE and Value 0 (consensus/bor/statefull/processor.go lines 71-80, params/config.go line 271), and CommitState sends it to the StateReceiver (consensus/bor/contract/client.go line 73). The StateReceiver accepts only that system address (System.sol lines 4-7) and forwards each record to its receiver as onStateReceive(uint256,bytes) with itself as msg.sender (StateReceiver.sol lines 42-57). No path names a sender chosen by Ethereum or Heimdall. The PoolManager has no fallback and no onStateReceive, so a record naming it as receiver reverts. Its nonce on Polygon is 1.",
 BOR+"consensus/bor/statefull/processor.go; "+BOR+"params/config.go; "+BOR+"consensus/bor/contract/client.go; "+GC+"StateReceiver.sol; "+GC+"System.sol"),
C([56],"L2","BNB Smart Chain: Parlia system transactions, who signs them and where they may go.",
 "Every system message is built with From header.Coinbase (parlia.go lines 1389, 2069, 2096, 2110, 2131, 2137-2146), the coinbase must equal the address recovered from the header seal (lines 996-998), and a transaction counts as a system transaction only if its target is one of the fourteen system contracts, its gas price is zero and its recovered sender is the coinbase (lines 323-335, 106-121). The sender is therefore a validator key and the target a fixed system contract. Even a validator cannot make it the PoolManager, because that would need the PoolManager's private key. The PoolManager's nonce is 1.",
 BSC),
C([43114],"L2","Avalanche C-Chain: atomic import and export, the native asset precompiles, and any other path whose sender is not a signer or calling frame.",
 "None reaches the EVM with a chosen sender. An import is an operation that mints to the named addresses and runs no EVM code (vms/saevm/cchain/tx/import.go lines 206-225, hooks.go lines 228-243). An export debits a C-Chain address only when a secp256k1 signature recovers to that address (export.go lines 175-197). nativeAssetCall used the precompile's caller as sender when it was active and is now DeprecatedContract, which reverts (graft/coreth/nativeasset/contract.go lines 176-180); an eth_call to 0x0100000000000000000000000000000000000002 and 0x0100000000000000000000000000000000000001 on avalanchego/1.15.3 reverted with empty data.",
 AV+"vms/saevm/cchain/tx/export.go; "+AV+"vms/saevm/cchain/tx/import.go; "+AV+"graft/coreth/nativeasset/contract.go; "+WD+"onchain_tests.json"),
C([143],"L2","Monad: system transactions and staking syscalls.",
 "A system transaction is accepted only when its recovered sender is SYSTEM_SENDER 0x6f49a8F621353f12378d0046E7d7e4b9B249DC9e, it is a legacy transaction, and its target is the staking precompile (validate_system_transaction.cpp lines 47, 51, 59); it then runs the native staking syscall, not EVM code at a chosen address (execute_system_transaction.cpp lines 178-182, 205-232). SYSTEM_SENDER cannot be an EIP-7702 authority (validate_monad_transaction.cpp line 53). The header comment calls it a key known to consensus (system_sender.hpp lines 22-25); I did not establish whether the key is public. In neither case can the sender be the PoolManager, whose nonce is 1.",
 MON+"monad/validate_system_transaction.cpp; "+MON+"monad/execute_system_transaction.cpp; "+MON+"monad/system_sender.hpp; "+MON+"monad/validate_monad_transaction.cpp"),
C([4326],"L2","MegaETH: deposits, mega system transactions, keyless deploys and pre-block system calls, for an ordinary account.",
 "An ordinary account cannot make any of them carry the PoolManager as sender. Deposits take from from the portal, which aliases contract callers (see the MegaETH finding of this unit for the governance path). Mega system transactions must be legacy transactions recovered to 0xA887dCB9D5f39Ef79272801d05Abdf707CFBbD1d and calling the Oracle contract (system/tx.rs lines 22-25, 40-70; execution.rs lines 104-110). Pre-block system calls use 0xff..fe (system/tx.rs lines 129-133). The keyless deploy signer is recovered from its signature (cleared in earlier cycles).",
 MEGA+"crates/mega-evm/src/system/tx.rs; "+MEGA+"crates/mega-evm/src/evm/execution.rs"),
C([4217],"L2","Tempo: system transactions, sub-blocks, fee payers, type 0x76 batches with keychain signatures, and TIP-1107 configurable accounts (T14, scheduled for 8 December 2026).",
 "None lets an ordinary account act as the PoolManager. A system transaction is a legacy transaction with signature (0,0) and sender Address::ZERO (envelope.rs lines 26-29, 234-236, 379-384), and the block executor accepts one only with target address zero carrying sub-block metadata, refusing every other target (block.rs lines 617-660); the committee system call uses caller zero and a fixed target (lines 589-591). Sub-block transactions are refused (lines 671-676; handler.rs line 1400). A fee payer signs separately and does not change the sender. A keychain signature names user_address, which must equal the recovered signer (handler.rs lines 667-676); a key authorization must be signed by that account or, from T6, by an admin key of it (handler.rs lines 566-573, 775-792); and the keychain precompile accepts authorizeKey only when msg.sender is tx.origin and the transaction is signed by the root key or an admin key (account_keychain/mod.rs lines 1193-1209). The PoolManager is never tx.origin, so no key can be registered for it. A configurable account address is the last 20 bytes of a keccak256 of its configuration (tip-1107.md lines 84-99) and must have empty code or the 0xef marker (line 171); the PoolManager has code, and matching its address would need a 160 bit preimage. Address zero, the protocol fee controller on Tempo, reaches only the sub-block metadata target and the committee contract.",
 TMP+"crates/primitives/src/transaction/envelope.rs; "+TMP+"crates/evm/src/block.rs; "+TMP+"crates/evm/src/handler.rs; "+TMP+"crates/precompiles/src/account_keychain/mod.rs; "+TMP+"tips/tip-1107.md"),
C([5042],"L2","Arc: who may name the sender of a CallFrom subcall, checked in source and on chain.",
 "Only Memo 0x5294E9927c3306DcBaDb03fe70b92e01cCede505 and Multicall3From 0x522fAf9A91c41c443c66765030741e4AaCe147D0 may call CallFrom (evm.rs lines 661-669, 1665-1680; execution-config call_from.rs), both pass their own msg.sender (Memo.sol line 43, Multicall3From.sol lines 43, 58, 81), and the EVM rejects a child caller that is neither the calling helper nor tx.origin (evm.rs lines 946-955). On arc/v1: an EOA calling CallFrom directly with the PoolManager as sender reverted with 'unauthorized caller'; an EOA calling Memo with a USDC transfer ran it as the EOA (reverted 'transfer amount exceeds balance'); a contract placed by state override calling Memo reverted with 'sender spoofing requires tx.origin as sender'. The PoolManager is never tx.origin.",
 ARC+"crates/evm/src/evm.rs; "+ARC+"contracts/src/memo/Memo.sol; "+WD+"onchain_tests.json; "+WD+"arc_stub_memo.json"),
C([999],"L2","HyperEVM: senders of system transactions in a fresh census, and whether an ordinary account can make HyperCore name the PoolManager.",
 "In blocks %d to %d (%d blocks, eth_getSystemTxsByBlockNumber on '%s') there were %d system transactions from %d senders: the HYPE bridge 0x2222222222222222222222222222222222222222, 25 token system addresses 0x20..., the CoreDepositWallet 0x6b9e773128f453f5c2c60935ee2de2cbc5390a24 (2,175 bytes of code) and the treasury 0xc20699185c15d0a2fd65779bb5d69f5b0b113c00. None names the PoolManager or its owner as sender, target or argument, HyperCore spotMeta links none of 195 linked tokens to either, and the PoolManager's nonce is 1. The archive client takes the sender from the upstream from field, falling back to 0x2222... for empty input and the token system address otherwise (nanoreth src/node/types/reth_compat.rs lines 236-249), and treats any zero gas price transaction as a system transaction (executor.rs lines 44-49), so the sender is whatever HyperCore states. That choice stays with HyperCore at operator level, recorded as F-020; an ordinary account cannot link a token to the PoolManager (cleared in cycle 4)." % (hl['range'][0],hl['range'][1],hl['range'][1]-hl['range'][0]+1,hl['client_start'],hl['systemTxs'],len(hl['senders'])),
 WD+"hl_systx_census.json; "+WD+"hl_spotMeta.json; "+NANO+"src/node/types/reth_compat.rs; "+NANO+"src/node/evm/executor.rs"),
C(NINE,"L2","Has any path that writes a sender outside a signature ever acted as the PoolManager on the nine networks?",
 "No evidence of it. eth_getTransactionCount of the PoolManager is 1 on all nine endpoints (Ethereum, Polygon, BNB Smart Chain, Avalanche, Monad, MegaETH, Tempo, Arc, HyperEVM), the value set at creation. Deposits, Parlia system transactions, Monad system transactions and HyperEVM system transactions bump the sender's nonce, so none of them has been sent in its name. Bor system messages and Tempo system transactions do not bump a nonce, but their senders are fixed constants.",
 WD+"pm_nonces.json"),
C(ALL,"L1","Signature checks that tokens or Permit2 could run on behalf of the PoolManager (EIP-2612 permit, ERC-1271).",
 "None can succeed. src/ has no isValidSignature function (no match for isValidSignature or 1271 in src/), so an ERC-1271 check against the PoolManager reverts or returns no magic value, and an ecrecover check would need its private key. The PoolManager also never approves a spender: its only outgoing calls are transfer, balanceOf, the hook selectors, unlockCallback and empty value calls (src/types/Currency.sol:40-80, src/PoolManager.sol:104-114).",
 "src/types/Currency.sol:40-80"),
C(ALL,"L1","What the code does when a call reaches it with msg.sender equal to the PoolManager itself.",
 "Nothing useful to an attacker. unlock calls IUnlockCallback(msg.sender).unlockCallback, and the PoolManager has no such function, so the call reverts (src/PoolManager.sol:104-114). take, settle, mint, burn, clear, swap, modifyLiquidity and donate require the lock (src/PoolManager.sol:96-99). The owner functions check msg.sender against the stored owner (lib/solmate/src/auth/Owned.sol:19-23) and fee collection against protocolFeeController (src/ProtocolFees.sol:44-57). The danger of a forged PoolManager sender lies in calls to tokens, which bypass all of this code.",
 "src/PoolManager.sol:104-114")]

leads=[{"chainIds":[4326],"lens":"L2","question":"Has the MegaETH OptimismPortal2 proxy 0x7f82f57F0Dd546519324392e408b01fcC7D709e8 ever pointed at an implementation other than 0x55400445e384393f9c1be23e7e734e8d44ed9fd9? Scan its Upgraded events from deployment.","why":"The public endpoints I tried refused archive or wide eth_getLogs ranges (publicnode needs a token, drpc allows 100 blocks, flashbots 2,000) and the Blockscout API timed out. A temporary implementation like the one OP Mainnet used on 2026-10-31 would show there.","priority":"medium"},
 {"chainIds":[143],"lens":"L2","question":"Is the private key of Monad SYSTEM_SENDER 0x6f49a8F621353f12378d0046E7d7e4b9B249DC9e public, and can an ordinary signed transaction from it call the staking precompile's reward, snapshot or epoch change functions?","why":"system_sender.hpp calls it a known key. It cannot be the PoolManager, but a public key would let anyone act as the address consensus uses for staking syscalls.","priority":"low"}]

